params: serviceId, version, sourceSha, sourceFilename, rawYaml, canonicalJson, effectiveJson, publishedBy = 8. Good.

Now `listAudit` — update SELECT to include justification and the AuditRow mapper (AuditRow now has justification before details). Current listAudit code:

```java
public List<AuditRow> listAudit(String service, Long attemptId) {
    StringBuilder sql = new StringBuilder("""
            SELECT id, service, version, attempt_id, action, decision, actor, details, created_at
            FROM audit_events WHERE (? IS NULL OR service = ?) AND (? IS NULL OR attempt_id = ?)
            ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 500
            """);
    return jdbc.query(sql.toString(), (rs, i) -> new AuditRow(rs.getLong("id"),
            rs.getString("service"), rs.getString("version"),
            rs.getObject("attempt_id") == null ? null : rs.getLong("attempt_id"),
            rs.getString("action"), rs.getString("decision"), rs.getString("actor"),
            rs.getString("details"), toInstant(rs.getTimestamp("created_at"))),
            service, service, attemptId, attemptId);
}
```

Also a subtle H2 issue: `? IS NULL OR service = ?` with String param — H2 typing of `? IS NULL` might be an issue but generally works with setNull-able params. Passing `service` (a String, possibly null) four times — JdbcTemplate sets null with SQL type UNKNOWN which usually works in H2. To be safe I could build SQL conditionally instead. Let me rewrite listAudit to build the WHERE clause conditionally — cleaner and avoids param typing issues:

```java
public List<AuditRow> listAudit(String service, Long attemptId) {
    StringBuilder sql = new StringBuilder("""
            SELECT id, service, version, attempt_id, action, decision, actor, justification, details, created_at
            FROM audit_events WHERE 1=1
            """);
    List<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
    if (service != null) { sql.append(" AND service = ?"); params.add(service); }
    if (attemptId != null) { sql.append(" AND attempt_id = ?"); params.add(attemptId); }
    sql.append(" ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 500");
    return jdbc.query(sql.toString(), (rs, i) -> new AuditRow(...), params.toArray());
}
```

Need `import java.util.ArrayList;` — check current imports in RegistryStore: Timestamp, Instant, List, Optional, JdbcTemplate, SimpleJdbcInsert (unused! I imported SimpleJdbcInsert but never used it — remove), GeneratedKeyHolder, KeyHolder, Repository, Transactional. I should remove SimpleJdbcInsert import and add ArrayList import.

Let me apply that edit.

