Two distinct problems:

1. **`notifications.status VARCHAR(32)` too short** — my failure string "failed: ResourceAccessException: ..." is 134 chars. The schema column is VARCHAR(32) (kit-given). Fix: truncate the status at write time in the notifier (status column stays small: "delivered" or "failed" + a SHORT classifier). Store concise status like `failed:ResourceAccessException` truncated to 32. Change `deliver()`: build status = "delivered" or "failed: " + simpleName, truncate to 32 chars.

2. **`SELECT ... FROM spec_versions` / `findings` StatementCallback failures** — likely also column-too-long? No — these are SELECTs... The message got truncated at display. "Value too long" can also apply to result mapping? No. StatementCallback failing on SELECT spec_versions — what error? Possibly "Column not found: published_by" — wait, published_by IS in schema now. Hmm — but the in-memory DB was created by schema.sql from THIS run... Unless the in-memory DB persisted from a previous test run in the same JVM?? No — fresh JVM per surefire run.

Wait — the test config `spring.sql.init.mode: always` runs schema.sql. But maybe the schema.sql on the classpath... the TEST classpath also has `target/classes/schema.sql` — updated. Hmm.

Actually the real cause might be the ALTER statements: `ALTER TABLE spec_versions ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS published_by VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'anonymous'` — H2 might not support NOT NULL DEFAULT in ALTER ADD COLUMN syntax and the whole schema.sql execution FAILED → some tables missing columns → subsequent SELECT published_by fails with "Column not found". The CREATE statements include the columns (fresh DB), so CREATEs define them; but if schema.sql aborted at the ALTER line (error), later CREATEs (audit_events!) never ran → audit SELECT would fail too... The audit Trail worked (Body shows audit rows returned!). audit_events table EXISTS and has justification — so schema.sql ran fully INCLUDING the ALTERs (audit_events is defined after the ALTERs). So ALTERs succeeded.

Then what fails in `SELECT version, source_sha, source_filename, state, published_at, published_by FROM spec_versions WHERE...`? The columns exist...

Hmm wait — look closer: the FIRST failure (autoPublishFlow) is the notifications insert blowing the transaction — that insert happens INSIDE... no wait, notifier.deliver is called AFTER the store transaction (autoPublishAttempt returns, then deliver). The insertNotification runs in its own implicit transaction. That threw DataIntegrityViolationException → propagated UP through deliver → caught? deliver catches Exception around the HTTP call and store.insertNotification — the catch wraps `client.post()...` AND `store.insertNotification(...)`?? Look at my deliver():

```java
try {
    client.post()...
    store.insertNotification(... "delivered" ...);
    LOG.info(...);
} catch (Exception e) {
    String status = "failed: " + ...;
    store.insertNotification(kind, service, version, payloadJson, targetUrl, status, null);  // ← this itself throws!
    LOG.warn(...);
}
```

If the HTTP call succeeds but the "delivered" insert... no. Flow: HTTP to localhost:9 fails → catch → insertNotification with the LONG status → throws DataIntegrityViolation → escapes deliver() → propagates up through gate() → upload() → controller → 500. THAT explains autoPublishFlow/duplicateSemantics(dup-identical? no wait — duplicateSemantics' 409 conflict → no notify; but the FIRST upload in duplicateSemantics is the identical re-upload → 200 no-op no notify... hmm, but duplicateSemantics got DataIntegrityViolation on notifications INSERT — because WIDGET_V2 upload in Order(2)... no wait, test ordering — duplicateSemantics runs after autoPublishFlow FAILED mid-way. State is messy from cascading failures: autoPublishFlow died on the V1 upload (notifications insert), so subsequent tests hit missing state: dryRunCheck failed on `SELECT ... spec_versions` — because currentVersion lookup? No...

Actually dryRunCheck's error: `StatementCallback; SQL [SELECT version, source_sha, ... FROM spec_versions WHERE service_id = ? AND state='published' ORDER BY published_at DESC LIMIT 1]` — statement fails. Why would a plain SELECT fail? H2 error likely "Column ... not found" or "Table not found"... OR — the H2 CONNECTION IS BROKEN: after a DataIntegrityViolation, H2 transactions roll back, but if the exception left the connection in a aborted state... With DEFAULT transaction isolation + @Transactional... Hmm.

More likely: "Value too long" aborted the transaction, and H2 marks the SESSION as transaction rollback-only? Subsequent statements on the SAME pooled connection fail with "Transaction was rolled back" or similar until pool discards it. Actually H2 throws "40188: Transaction was rolled back"... The truncated output doesn't show the cause for the SELECT failures.

Root cause #1 fix (truncate status) likely fixes everything downstream too (the cascade). Let me also make the failure path robust: move the "delivered" insert INTO the try and the failed-insert into a nested try-catch so logging never throws:

```java
public void deliver(...) {
    String status;
    Instant deliveredAt;
    try {
        client.post()...;
        status = "delivered";
        deliveredAt = Instant.now();
    } catch (Exception e) {
        status = shortStatus(e);   // <= 32 chars
        deliveredAt = null;
    }
    try {
        store.insertNotification(kind, service, version, payloadJson, targetUrl, status, deliveredAt);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        LOG.warn("could not record notification: {}", e.toString());
    }
}
```

shortStatus: `"failed: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName()` → e.g. "failed: ResourceAccessException" = 31 chars — exactly fits 32. Truncate defensively to 32: status.substring(0, Math.min(32, status.length())).

Also improve the message detail? The status column is only 32 — keep "failed: <SimpleClassName>" and put NOTHING more. Fine for the log table.

Also `dryRunCheck`/`invalidSpecRejection`/`acknowledgeFlow` SELECT failures — presumably cascade from broken connections; after fixing the insert, re-run and see. If SELECTs still fail, I'll dig deeper (could be the `LIMIT 1` in currentVersion with H2 needing `LIMIT` syntax — H2 2.x supports LIMIT in SELECT... yes H2 supports LIMIT. But actually — wait, H2 2.x in REGULAR mode supports `LIMIT`? H2 2.x supports `FETCH FIRST n ROWS ONLY` AND still accepts LIMIT. Yes, LIMIT works in H2 2.x.

Also the failing SELECT in acknowledgeFlow: `SELECT id, code, severity, ... FROM findings WHERE attempt_id = ?` — again likely cascade.

One more possible root cause: my `listVersions` SELECT includes `published_by` — schema has it. OK.

Also autoPublishFlow's V1 upload: the notification insert happened INSIDE the switch AFTER autoPublishAttempt committed... the version WAS committed (attempt 1 + version rows visible in later audit dump: SPEC_UPLOADED id 3 auto_published alice 1.0.0 → transaction committed; notification failed after). So the cascade theory holds: subsequent operations hit a poisoned pooled connection (H2 default: after an exception on a connection, the transaction is rolled back; but since this insert was in autocommit mode... whatever — fix and re-run).

Fix WebhookNotifier now.

