Hmm — wait. I moved the guarded UPDATE to the END (after audit + spec insert). Reasoning: the guard must fail the transaction AFTER we know the update lost... no wait, that's wrong reasoning. Original order: transition → UPDATE → audit → spec. If the guarded update is LAST and fails, everything rolls back ✓. If it's FIRST and fails, transaction rolls back via exception ✓ same atomicity. But there's a subtle issue with putting it LAST: under a concurrent race, we'd supersede the currently-published spec_version and INSERT a new version row for a version that was concurrently decided... but the whole transaction rolls back on the guard failure, so the supersede+insert are undone ✓. Atomicity holds either way.

But there's another subtlety with putting the guarded update last: two concurrent transactions both do spec-version work; the loser rolls back — fine. However H2 default lock behavior: both transactions could hold row locks... T1 updates attempt row state (write lock on that row); T2's guarded UPDATE blocks until T1 commits, then sees state != previousState → 0 rows → rollback ✓. That's exactly the serialization we want. With the update FIRST, T2 would block at the start (same result). Either order works; LAST is fine and keeps the transition-first rationale ("from_state is the genuine previous state" — since from_state comes from the caller's earlier read... actually under concurrency the read could be stale; the guard catches it ✓).

Hmm — one more ordering consideration: putting the attempt UPDATE last means the spec_versions insert happens before the attempt state update within the tx — no observable difference (atomic commit).

Wait, actually there IS one more subtle correctness point in favor of the update being last: if the guarded UPDATE were FIRST and the spec_versions insert then hit a unique constraint (version already registered somehow), the tx rolls back ✓ same. OK, order is fine.

Also — I removed the void return; now boolean. PublicationService ignores it? The throw handles the race (rollback + exception propagates to controller → 500 unless mapped). Hmm — I threw IllegalStateException; the controller maps unknown exceptions how? Let me check the @ExceptionHandler in RegistryController — there's probably a catch-all → 500. A lost race yielding 500 is ugly; better 409 ConflictException. But RegistryStore is persistence-layer; importing com.contractguard.api.ConflictException there is a layering smell... but the api package is the app's exception vocabulary (like NotFoundException used in PublicationService — pipeline imports api exceptions ✓ precedent exists). For the store, cleaner: throw Spring's `org.springframework.dao.OptimisticLockingFailureException`? Is there a mapping? Probably not; falls to catch-all 500.

Decision: keep IllegalStateException in the store? Or make the store NOT throw and return false, letting PublicationService throw ConflictException → 409. That's cleaner layering and better UX. The throw-inside-store guarantees rollback via exception; returning false ALSO needs rollback of the writes done so far (transition + audit + spec rows) — with return false, the transaction COMMITS unless something throws! That's the trap: if decideAndPublish returns false after writing the transition/audit/spec rows, @Transactional commits those writes! That would leave a transition row + superseded published version WITHOUT the attempt state change — a partial commit. So the store MUST throw to roll back. Keep IllegalStateException in the store.

Then PublicationService.decide: catch nothing — let it propagate. Map IllegalStateException → 409? The controller's exception handlers: let me check what handlers exist in RegistryController/ApiException setup. If there's a generic handler mapping unexpected exceptions to 500, the concurrent-decide race shows 500 — acceptable? The task says "wrong-state decisions→400" and duplicates→409. A concurrent race isn't in the enumerated API semantics. But we can do better without layering damage: PublicationService.decide() can pre-catch... no — it can't distinguish. Alternative: PublicationService calls decideAndPublish and on IllegalStateException (guard) rethrows ConflictException. But catching IllegalStateException is risky (other causes — e.g., missing service slug? serviceSlug returns Optional "(unknown)" fallback... the "UPDATE spec_versions" can't throw ISE; insertGated... not in this method). The only ISE source in decideAndPublish is the guard ✓. But catch-and-rethrow inside the same transaction boundary: the store method is @Transactional (REQUIRED); the exception marks rollback-only on the store's tx? With default propagation REQUIRED and the controller NOT having a tx, the store's tx is the outermost — an exception thrown from the store's proxied method marks it rollback-only AND the tx completes (rollback) when the proxy exits the store method. By the time PublicationService catches, the store tx has already rolled back ✓. Then rethrowing ConflictException gives 409 ✓ clean.

Hmm wait — actually with @Transactional on the store method, when the method throws, the proxy completes the rollback BEFORE returning the exception to the caller ✓. So catching in PublicationService is safe and clean. Let me do that in decide():

```java
        try {
            store.decideAndPublish(...);
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            throw new ConflictException("attempt was decided concurrently; reload and retry: "
                    + e.getMessage());
        }
```

Hmm — is ConflictException's message okay? Check ConflictException constructor (takes message ✓ presumably).

Wait — one more issue: I catch IllegalStateException — but specParser.parse removed from decide... let me now rewrite PublicationService.decide + gate call sites.

Also add decided_at for auto_published/rejected inserts.

