Wait, there's a flawed assertion in `paramSerializationSemantics`:

```java
.allSatisfy(f -> {
    assertThat(f.location()).contains("param:query:1").orSatisfies(
            l -> assertThat(l).contains("param:query:2"));
});
```
`assertThat(String).contains(...)` returns an AbstractStringAssert — `.orSatisfies` isn't a chainable method there... Actually AssertJ: `assertThat(x).contains(a).orSatisfies(...)`? AbstractObjectAssert has `.returns`, and `AbstractIterableAssert`... For string asserts, `.contains()` returns AbstractStringAssert which extends AbstractCharSequenceAssert — there's `satisfies(Consumer)` on AbstractAssert but chaining `.contains(...).orSatisfies(...)` — hmm, `orSatisfies` does exist? There's `satisfies` on AbstractAssert... I don't think `orSatisfies` exists in AssertJ 3. There IS `hasFieldOrPropertyWithValue`... Let me simplify: just assert each filtered sub-list separately:

```java
assertThat(findings).filteredOn(f -> f.code().equals("PARAM.SERIALIZATION_CHANGED")).hasSize(2);
assertThat(findings).extracting(CompatibilityFinding::location).contains("... :: param:query:1", "... :: param:query:2");
```
Wait locations are full keys "GET /a :: param:query:1". Extracting locations and asserting contains both strings — simpler and robust:

```java
assertThat(findings).filteredOn(f -> f.code().equals("PARAM.SERIALIZATION_CHANGED"))
        .hasSize(2)
        .extracting(CompatibilityFinding::location)
        .containsExactlyInAnyOrder("GET /a :: param:query:1", "GET /a :: param:query:2");
```
Hmm — what's the exact location string? The locationKey is `"GET /a :: param:query:1"` (EffectiveLocation.key()). And `displayPath` shows raw path "/a". So location = "GET /a :: param:query:1". Contains-exact match works. But safer: `.contains("param:query:1", ...)` with extracting → contains checks collection contains exact elements; the elements are full keys. Use `.allMatch(l -> l.contains("param:query:"))` plus count. Simplest robust:

```java
.hasSize(2)
.extracting(CompatibilityFinding::location)
.allSatisfy(l -> assertThat(l).contains("param:query:"));
```
plus severity split assertions (already separate). But then I lose the ordinal pinning... The BREAKING assertion pins param:query:1 and INFO pins param:query:2 — that covers ordinals. So the first allSatisfy can just assert code+size. Let me fix that assertion.

